{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EAowK7F2nUMPHXFjymeXz7","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Review Form Summary\n\nBased on the provided content, **a complete review form with numeric scoring scales and evaluation dimensions is not included** in this document.\n\n## What Is Provided:\n\n**Review Criteria for Regular Papers:**\nThe document states that submissions will be evaluated on:\n- \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\"\n- How well results are \"objectively established\" and their \"potential for scientific and technological impact\"\n- Relevance to robotic learning\n\n## What Is Missing:\n\nThe page does not contain:\n- A numbered or lettered rating scale (e.g., 1-5, 1-10)\n- Specific score definitions (what constitutes \"strong accept\" vs. \"weak reject\")\n- Quantitative evaluation dimensions\n- A structured review form template\n- Weighting guidance for different criteria\n\n## Additional Information Available:\n\nThe document does specify **Desk Reject Criteria** (formatting, anonymity violations, scope relevance) and **Reviewer Qualifications** (minimum 3 first-author publications in major robotics/ML venues recommended).\n\nTo access the actual review form and scoring rubric, you would likely need to consult the full CoRL 2024 submission system or contact the organizing committee directly."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1a54116-c461-4e8a-8039-725147cd9263","uuid":"fe461c5c-fc26-477d-adf9-1743d1403c78","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:53:29.798Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":133965,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Review Form Summary\n\nBased on the provided content, **a complete review form with numeric scoring scales and evaluation dimensions is not included** in this document.\n\n## What Is Provided:\n\n**Review Criteria for Regular Papers:**\nThe document states that submissions will be evaluated on:\n- \"significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\"\n- How well results are \"objectively established\" and their \"potential for scientific and technological impact\"\n- Relevance to robotic learning\n\n## What Is Missing:\n\nThe page does not contain:\n- A numbered or lettered rating scale (e.g., 1-5, 1-10)\n- Specific score definitions (what constitutes \"strong accept\" vs. \"weak reject\")\n- Quantitative evaluation dimensions\n- A structured review form template\n- Weighting guidance for different criteria\n\n## Additional Information Available:\n\nThe document does specify **Desk Reject Criteria** (formatting, anonymity violations, scope relevance) and **Reviewer Qualifications** (minimum 3 first-author publications in major robotics/ML venues recommended).\n\nTo access the actual review form and scoring rubric, you would likely need to consult the full CoRL 2024 submission system or contact the organizing committee directly.","durationMs":4630,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pb4wN6WYoPVEp2tTxMYpRs","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Review Guidelines Summary\n\nBased on the provided content, here's what's available:\n\n## Evaluation Dimensions\n\nThe guidelines specify six key assessment areas:\n\n1. **Originality** - \"Are the problems or methods new?\" Novel combinations and clear differentiation from prior work matter.\n\n2. **Quality** - Technical soundness, evidence supporting claims, appropriate methods, and honest evaluation of strengths/weaknesses.\n\n3. **Clarity** - Clear writing, good organization, and sufficient detail for expert reproducibility.\n\n4. **Significance** - Importance of results and potential impact on the field and practitioners.\n\n5. **Relevance** - \"Does the paper address a problem in robot learning?\" and audience interest.\n\n6. **Limitations** - Papers must include limitations sections; honestly reported ones should be treated favorably.\n\n## Missing Information\n\n**The document does NOT contain:**\n- A complete review form template\n- Specific numeric scoring scales or rating ranges\n- What each score number means (e.g., 1-5, 1-10)\n- Acceptance rate statistics\n- Detailed scoring rubrics with point values\n\nThe guidelines emphasize constructive feedback and avoiding common reviewer mistakes but don't provide the actual scoring mechanism reviewers use when submitting evaluations."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1a54116-c461-4e8a-8039-725147cd9263","uuid":"d8bdd809-ee65-45a6-9d28-3e509aeeb267","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:53:30.894Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Review Guidelines Summary\n\nBased on the provided content, here's what's available:\n\n## Evaluation Dimensions\n\nThe guidelines specify six key assessment areas:\n\n1. **Originality** - \"Are the problems or methods new?\" Novel combinations and clear differentiation from prior work matter.\n\n2. **Quality** - Technical soundness, evidence supporting claims, appropriate methods, and honest evaluation of strengths/weaknesses.\n\n3. **Clarity** - Clear writing, good organization, and sufficient detail for expert reproducibility.\n\n4. **Significance** - Importance of results and potential impact on the field and practitioners.\n\n5. **Relevance** - \"Does the paper address a problem in robot learning?\" and audience interest.\n\n6. **Limitations** - Papers must include limitations sections; honestly reported ones should be treated favorably.\n\n## Missing Information\n\n**The document does NOT contain:**\n- A complete review form template\n- Specific numeric scoring scales or rating ranges\n- What each score number means (e.g., 1-5, 1-10)\n- Acceptance rate statistics\n- Detailed scoring rubrics with point values\n\nThe guidelines emphasize constructive feedback and avoiding common reviewer mistakes but don't provide the actual scoring mechanism reviewers use when submitting evaluations.","durationMs":5019,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
