{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019g3dS37DRNwaX8ifv9GvaM","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CoRL OpenReview review form \"overall score\" rating scale 1-10 accept reject\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Reviewer Guidelines — CoRL 2023\",\"url\":\"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Default Review Form | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://docs.openreview.net/reference/default-forms/default-review-form\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2026 - Instruction for Authors\",\"url\":\"https://www.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-authors\"},{\"title\":\"An Open Review of OpenReview: A Critical Analysis of the Machine   Learning Conference Review Process\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.05137\"},{\"title\":\"OpenReviewer: A Specialized Large Language Model for Generating Critical Scientific Paper Reviews\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.11948v3\"},{\"title\":\"Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2021 AN OPEN REVIEW OF OPENREVIEW:\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=Cn706AbJaKW\"},{\"title\":\"What Have We Learned from OpenReview?\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.05885\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the OpenReview default review form rating scale. The rating scale uses values from 1 to 10, with descriptions including: \"10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\", \"9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\", \"8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\", \"7: Good paper, accept\", \"6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\", \"5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\", \"4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\", \"3: Clear rejection\", \"2: Strong rejection\", and \"1: Trivial or wrong\".\n\nThis is the standard OpenReview form structure. While my search results included CoRL reviewer guidelines, they didn't contain the specific CoRL review form template details. The default OpenReview form shown above is commonly used across many conferences on the OpenReview platform.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1a54116-c461-4e8a-8039-725147cd9263","uuid":"2c16b3f5-9476-4136-a989-31106cf86f49","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:53:41.541Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CoRL OpenReview review form \"overall score\" rating scale 1-10 accept reject","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01A5B5EHM4V6UaycEYV2Fu6D","content":[{"title":"Reviewer Guidelines — CoRL 2023","url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"},{"title":"Default Review Form | OpenReview","url":"https://docs.openreview.net/reference/default-forms/default-review-form"},{"title":"CoRL 2026 - Instruction for Authors","url":"https://www.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-authors"},{"title":"An Open Review of OpenReview: A Critical Analysis of the Machine   Learning Conference Review Process","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.05137"},{"title":"OpenReviewer: A Specialized Large Language Model for Generating Critical Scientific Paper Reviews","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.11948v3"},{"title":"Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2021 AN OPEN REVIEW OF OPENREVIEW:","url":"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=Cn706AbJaKW"},{"title":"What Have We Learned from OpenReview?","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.05885"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about the OpenReview default review form rating scale. The rating scale uses values from 1 to 10, with descriptions including: \"10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\", \"9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\", \"8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\", \"7: Good paper, accept\", \"6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\", \"5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\", \"4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\", \"3: Clear rejection\", \"2: Strong rejection\", and \"1: Trivial or wrong\".\n\nThis is the standard OpenReview form structure. While my search results included CoRL reviewer guidelines, they didn't contain the specific CoRL review form template details. The default OpenReview form shown above is commonly used across many conferences on the OpenReview platform."],"durationSeconds":6.1648143320000015}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Nz7mceh2mh1PqufQXxV1En","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the CoRL review criteria and the OpenReview default scoring scale. Let me also fetch the OpenReview default review form to confirm the exact scale.\n\nI now have enough context to proceed:\n\n**CoRL Review Criteria** (from CoRL 2023 guidelines):\n1. Originality\n2. Quality\n3. Clarity\n4. Significance\n5. Relevance\n6. Limitations\n\n**OpenReview default scale** (1-10):\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n\n\n- 9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\n- 3: Clear rejection\n- 2: Strong rejection\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nWith CoRL's typical 30-35% acceptance rate in mind, I'll assign two reviewers with complementary expertise—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then facilitate a structured discussion starting with independent evaluations before they exchange perspectives.\n\nI need to prepare the paper summary and review context for both agents. The paper investigates why pixel-shuffle connectors in SmolVLA completely fail under token pruning while linear projections remain robust, proposes a diagnostic method based on computer vision principles that predicts pruning tolerance with a single forward pass, and explores recovery mechanisms. R (Vision Token Re-encoding) recovers pixel-shuffle VLAs from 0% to 60%+, VTR fails completely on MLP-projector VLAs, and there's a significant ~17pp gap between L2-norm and random pruning on OpenVLA with notable seed sensitivity showing 28pp variance. The experiments represent pessimistic outcomes, including OpenVLA's L2-norm pruning sweep, X-VLA cross-seed variance, and SmolVLA's flat M-curve, while RoboFlamingo didn't finish in time. I'm framing this as a cautionary tale that also serves as a diagnostic tool for understanding where these methods break down.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":633,"cache_read_input_tokens":54801,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":633},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1a54116-c461-4e8a-8039-725147cd9263","uuid":"683540cc-5b7b-494d-9b05-d83362951a28"}
