{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011V3N6B6sdtzYSSVrmqeJLx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Results}\n2\t\\label{sec:results}\n3\t\n4\tWe evaluate token pruning across five VLA models spanning three connector families on LIBERO-Object~\\cite{liu2023libero} (10 tasks, 50 episodes each, 500 total). All success rates include Wilson 95\\% confidence intervals for single-seed results and mean $\\pm$ standard deviation for multi-seed results.\n5\t\n6\t\\subsection{Taxonomy Overview}\n7\t\\label{sec:taxonomy_overview}\n8\t\n9\tTable~\\ref{tab:taxonomy} summarizes the five models and their pruning responses. Effective vision-to-LLM pipeline complexity, rather than the nominal connector label, determines pruning tolerance: pure linear projection ($\\pi_0$) is immune, linear projection + deep encoder (X-VLA) is threshold-graded, MLP degrades smoothly, and pixel-shuffle collapses catastrophically. Token importance CV tracks this ordering continuously (0.038, 0.151, 0.215, 0.314) across a 16$\\times$ parameter range (450M--7B). A controlled comparison between $\\pi_0$ and OpenVLA---both using 256 SigLIP~\\cite{zhai2023siglip} tokens, differing only in connector---yields a 63\\,pp gap at $k{=}0.1$, isolating the pipeline as causal (Section~\\ref{sec:linear_results}).\n10\t\n11\t\\begin{table}[t]\n12\t\\centering\n13\t\\small\n14\t\\caption{Five-model connector taxonomy. $^\\dagger\\pi_0$ reports offline normalized L1 action loss (pending closed-loop evaluation). $^\\ddagger$X-VLA uses a linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder; the effective pipeline is not purely linear. $^\\S$OFT reports attention entropy only (no pruning evaluation); entropy match with standard OpenVLA confirms the connector determines attention structure.}\n15\t\\label{tab:taxonomy}\n16\t\\begin{tabular}{llcrll}\n17\t\\toprule\n18\tModel & Connector & Params & Tokens & Representative Result & Class \\\\\n19\t\\midrule\n20\t$\\pi_0$~\\cite{black2024pi0} & Linear proj & 3.3B & 256 & $k{=}0.1$: $\\mathcal{L}_1{=}0.976$ ($+$2.4\\%)$^\\dagger$ & Immune (offline) \\\\\n21\tX-VLA~\\cite{zheng2025xvla} & Linear proj$^\\ddagger$ & 880M & 50 & $k{=}0.9$: $97.8{\\pm}0.4$, $k{=}0.7$: $57.2{\\pm}1.6$ & Thresh.-graded \\\\\n22\tOpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} & 3-layer MLP & 7B & 256 & $k{=}0.5$: 37.4\\% SR ($-$49\\%) & Smooth graded \\\\\n23\tOFT~\\cite{kim2025openoft} & 3-layer MLP & 7B & 256 & Ent.\\ $0.667 \\approx 0.661$ (Std)$^\\S$ & Smooth graded \\\\\n24\tSmolVLA~\\cite{shukor2025smolvla} & Pixel-shuffle & 450M & 128 & $k{=}0.95$: 0\\% SR & Catastrophic cliff \\\\\n25\t\\bottomrule\n26\t\\end{tabular}\n27\t\\end{table}\n28\t\n29\t\\subsection{Pixel-Shuffle: Catastrophic Cliff-Edge}\n30\t\\label{sec:smolvla_results}\n31\t\n32\tThe SmolVLA baseline (bypass mode, all 128 vision tokens) achieves 44.8\\% SR (224/500, CI [40.5\\%, 49.2\\%]).\\footnote{The official SmolVLA result is 87.3\\% SR with batch size 64 on 8 GPUs. Our reproduction uses a smaller batch due to compute constraints.} Every pruning configuration produces 0\\% SR across seven configurations varying scoring (learned ACIS vs.\\ random), retention ratio ($k \\in \\{0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\\}$), and mechanism (zero-masking, true removal, token merging); full ablation in Table~\\ref{tab:smolvla_ablation}, Appendix~\\ref{app:smolvla_ablation}. The official SmolVLA checkpoint (59.2\\% SR) exhibits identical failure, confirming the cliff-edge is architectural. The pattern generalizes across LIBERO suites (Spatial: 0.6\\%, Goal: 0\\%).\n33\t\n34\t\\subsection{MLP Projector: Graded Degradation}\n35\t\\label{sec:openvla_results}\n36\t\n37\tUnder random token removal, OpenVLA degrades gracefully across all retention ratios: 69.4\\% SR at $k{=}0.95$ ($-4.6$pp from the 74.0\\% baseline), 62.0\\% at $k{=}0.90$, 49.0\\% at $k{=}0.70$, 37.4\\% at $k{=}0.50$, and 20.6\\% at $k{=}0.30$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:results_combined}a; full results with CIs in Table~\\ref{tab:openvla_full}, Appendix). At $k{=}0.30$, two tasks (cream\\_cheese, butter) reach 0\\% SR, but eight retain non-zero performance: degradation is graded, not cliff-edge. Per-task breakdowns are in Appendix~\\ref{app:per_task}. Random token removal provides a conservative lower bound on architectural resilience; learned scoring methods would likely yield better retention.\n38\t\n39\tOFT~\\cite{kim2025openoft} retains OpenVLA's MLP connector but replaces the action head; its attention entropy (0.667) matches standard OpenVLA (0.661), confirming the connector, not the action head, determines token importance structure. EfficientVLA~\\cite{yang2025efficientvla} corroborates the graded pattern: CogACT~\\cite{li2024cogact} (MLP projector) retains 74.2\\% SR under 78\\% pruning.\n40\t\n41\t\\subsection{Linear Projection: From Immune to Threshold-Graded}\n42\t\\label{sec:linear_results}\n43\t\n44\t$\\pi_0$~\\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B, 256 tokens, linear projection) is evaluated via offline normalized L1 action loss.\\footnote{Offline action loss measures single-step prediction quality; closed-loop evaluation may reveal additional degradation. X-VLA's offline-to-closed-loop gap (Section~\\ref{sec:metric_disconnect}) underscores the need for live verification.} At $k{=}0.1$, the loss is 0.976---a 2.4\\% \\emph{improvement} over the unpruned baseline. At $k{=}0.5$ the loss remains 0.983.\n45\t\n46\tX-VLA~\\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M, 50 tokens) routes vision tokens through a 12-layer BART encoder~\\cite{lewis2020bart} before the LLM. Under L2 norm-based removal, the 99.2\\% baseline holds at $k{=}0.9$ ($97.8 \\pm 0.4$\\%, 3 seeds) but cliffs at $k{=}0.7$ ($57.2 \\pm 1.6$\\%, $-41$pp), then degrades further: $43.7 \\pm 1.0$\\% at $k{=}0.5$ (3 seeds), 23.0\\% at $k{=}0.3$, near-zero at $k{=}0.1$. The threshold lies in the $k{=}0.7$--$0.9$ interval, corresponding to retaining 35--45 of 50 BART-encoded tokens; below this density, cross-token dependencies established by the 12-layer encoder cannot be maintained. The cliff is reproducible across 3 seeds (std 1.6pp) despite extreme per-task variance (cream\\_cheese: $k{=}0.9{=}100$\\% vs.\\ $k{=}0.7{=}0$\\%). That this threshold appears under importance-based removal (the more favorable test) while OpenVLA degrades smoothly under random removal (the harder test) strengthens the architecture-dependent conclusion. The $\\pi_0$--X-VLA contrast shows the connector label alone is insufficient: effective pipeline complexity determines pruning tolerance.\n47\t\n48\t\\subsection{VTR: Re-Encoding Overcomes Cliff-Edge}\n49\t\\label{sec:smart_compress_results}\n50\t\n51\tTo test whether learned re-encoding can overcome the pixel-shuffle cliff-edge, we insert a 22.2M-parameter cross-attention module (Vision Token Re-encoder, VTR) between the vision connector and the LLM backbone (details in Appendix~\\ref{app:smart_compress_details}). VTR compresses 128 input tokens to $M$ output tokens. Both VTR and the baseline train from the same SmolVLA-Base checkpoint for 100K steps with identical hyperparameters. An extended training control (same procedure without the compressor) achieves 45.0\\% SR ($p > 0.9$ vs.\\ the 44.8\\% baseline), which confirms the gain is architectural, not from additional optimization steps.\\footnote{The extended baseline also collapses to 0\\% SR under pruning ($k{=}0.50$, random, true removal), which confirms that additional training does not mitigate the pixel-shuffle vulnerability.}\n52\t\n53\t\\begin{table}[t]\n54\t\\centering\n55\t\\small\n56\t\\caption{VTR re-encoding vs.\\ deletion on SmolVLA (LIBERO-Object). The M-curve is flat: $M{=}16$ (87.5\\% compression) matches $M{=}128$ (no compression) within 1.1pp. A parameter-matched MLP adapter achieves comparable SR ($p = 0.15$ vs.\\ cross-attention). $^\\ddagger$Mean $\\pm$ std over 3 seeds (500 episodes each). Other rows: single seed with Wilson 95\\% CI.}\n57\t\\label{tab:smart_compress}\n58\t\\begin{tabular}{lccrr}\n59\t\\toprule\n60\tMethod & $M$ & Red. & SR (\\%) & Seeds \\\\\n61\t\\midrule\n62\t\\textit{Baseline} & 128 & 0\\% & 44.8 & 1 \\\\\n63\t\\textit{Ext.\\ training} & -- & -- & 45.0 & 1 \\\\\n64\t\\textit{Hard pruning} & -- & 50--90\\% & 0.0 & mult. \\\\\n65\t\\midrule\n66\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 16 & 87.5\\% & 56.9$\\pm$1.3 & 55.8/58.4/56.5 \\\\\n67\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 32 & 75\\% & 60.1$\\pm$1.3 & 61.6/59.2/59.6 \\\\\n68\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 64 & 50\\% & \\textbf{62.0}$\\pm$2.3 & 61.6/60.0/64.4 \\\\\n69\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 96 & 25\\% & 54.5$\\pm$3.2 & 52.6/58.2/52.8 \\\\\n70\tCross-attn$^\\ddagger$ & 128 & 0\\% & 58.0$\\pm$3.5 & 59.8/54.0/60.2 \\\\\n71\tMLP adapter$^\\ddagger$ & 32 & 75\\% & 63.4$\\pm$2.6 & 65.2/64.6/60.4 \\\\\n72\t\\midrule\n73\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textit{Official checkpoint (59.2\\% baseline)}} \\\\\n74\tCross-attn & 32 & 75\\% & 53.4 & 1 \\\\\n75\t\\bottomrule\n76\t\\end{tabular}\n77\t\\end{table}\n78\t\n79\tThe M-curve is flat (Table~\\ref{tab:smart_compress}): $M{=}16$ (56.9\\%, 87.5\\% compression) performs within 1.1pp of $M{=}128$ (58.0\\%, no compression), and all five $M$ values cluster within 5pp. A parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters) achieves 63.4\\% $\\pm$ 2.6 at $M{=}32$ ($p = 0.15$ vs.\\ cross-attention), confirming re-encoding itself, not the attention mechanism, is the operative factor (Figure~\\ref{fig:results_combined}b). Cross-suite evaluation confirms generalization: 53.2\\% on LIBERO-Spatial and 56.4\\% on LIBERO-Goal where hard pruning yields 0\\% (Appendix~\\ref{app:smart_compress_full}). Applying VTR to OpenVLA yields 0\\% SR even in passthrough mode ($M{=}128$), indicating VTR is a targeted remedy for pixel-shuffle entanglement, not a universal compression module.\n80\t\n81\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n82\t\\centering\n83\t\\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\linewidth}\n84\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_degradation_curve.pdf}\n85\t\\caption{Token pruning across connector classes.}\n86\t\\label{fig:degradation_curve}\n87\t\\end{subfigure}\n88\t\\hfill\n89\t\\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\linewidth}\n90\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_compression_curve.pdf}\n91\t\\caption{VTR M-curve on SmolVLA.}\n92\t\\label{fig:compression_curve}\n93\t\\end{subfigure}\n94\t\\caption{Architecture-dependent response to token reduction on LIBERO-Object. (a)~Token pruning across pipeline types: pure linear projection ($\\pi_0$) is immune, linear+encoder (X-VLA) is threshold-graded (onset at $k{=}0.7$), MLP projector (OpenVLA) degrades gracefully, pixel-shuffle (SmolVLA) collapses. (b)~VTR M-curve: re-encoding achieves near-lossless compression ($M{=}16 \\approx M{=}128$) where deletion yields 0\\%.}\n95\t\\label{fig:results_combined}\n96\t\\end{figure}\n97\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PV6t3PHMrBdJoXswNLqQFg","session_id":"b1a54116-c461-4e8a-8039-725147cd9263","uuid":"7b512d72-490a-4746-8b91-02123099c163","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:55:39.211Z"}
