{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016b8fHy3jxi5p5BkPxxpGNT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{authblk}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\n14\t\\definecolor{immune}{RGB}{34,139,34}\n15\t\\definecolor{graded}{RGB}{255,165,0}\n16\t\\definecolor{catastrophic}{RGB}{220,20,60}\n17\t\\definecolor{threshold}{RGB}{70,130,180}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others:\\\\The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility}\n20\t\n21\t\\author[1]{Anonymous Authors}\n22\t\\affil[1]{Anonymous Institution}\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t%% ============================================================\n30\t%% ABSTRACT\n31\t%% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models process hundreds of visual tokens per frame, creating a bottleneck for real-time robotic deployment. While inference-time token reduction methods have shown promise, their effectiveness varies dramatically across architectures for reasons that remain unexplained. We present the first systematic study of token pruning robustness across VLA architectures, testing 6 models spanning 4 connector families and model sizes from 450M to 7B parameters. We discover that the \\emph{effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline}---not model size---determines pruning tolerance along a continuous spectrum. Pure linear projection ($\\pi_0$) is pruning-immune (loss \\emph{improves} 2.4\\% at 90\\% token removal). Linear projection followed by deep post-processing (X-VLA, 12-layer BART encoder) exhibits a threshold-graded pattern: robust at $k \\geq 0.9$ (97.6\\% SR) but cliffs at $k=0.7$ (56.2\\%). Learnable cross-attention queries (RoboFlamingo, Perceiver Resampler) show gentle graded degradation. Multi-layer MLP connectors (OpenVLA) degrade smoothly ($k=0.5 \\to 37.4\\%$ SR). Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA) collapse catastrophically---removing just 5\\% of tokens yields 0\\% success rate. The coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance scores predicts this spectrum: $\\text{CV} = 0.038$ (immune) $\\to 0.11$ (gentle) $\\to 0.151$ (threshold) $\\to 0.215$ (smooth) $\\to 0.314$ (catastrophic). Based on this analysis, we propose a Diagnose-then-Compress framework: (1) a zero-cost CV diagnostic that predicts pruning tolerance from a single forward pass, and (2) Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module ($\\sim$22M parameters) that provides universal token compression across connector types---recovering pixel-shuffle from 0\\% to 60.1\\% SR at 75\\% compression, and achieving 45--58\\% SR on MLP projectors at 75--87.5\\% compression where hard pruning retains only 8--21\\%.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t%% ============================================================\n37\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n38\t%% ============================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\n41\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as a promising paradigm for generalist robot policies, unifying vision understanding and action generation in a single transformer architecture~\\cite{brohan2023rt2,kim2024openvla,black2024pi0}. However, VLAs inherit a critical efficiency bottleneck from their VLM backbones: the vision encoder produces hundreds of tokens per frame (e.g., 256 for OpenVLA, 128 for SmolVLA), which dominate the LLM's key-value cache and attention computation during autoregressive action decoding.\n42\t\n43\tA growing literature proposes inference-time token reduction to address this bottleneck. Methods such as FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O, and ToMe have demonstrated that significant portions of visual tokens can be removed with minimal performance loss on certain architectures. However, these results are architecture-specific: each method is typically evaluated on a single VLA, and the implicit assumption that token pruning generalizes across architectures remains untested.\n44\t\n45\tWe challenge this assumption through the first systematic cross-architecture study of VLA token compressibility. Testing 6 VLA models spanning 4 connector families and sizes from 450M to 7B parameters on the LIBERO manipulation benchmark, we discover that pruning tolerance is determined by the \\emph{effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline}---the complete pathway from vision encoder output to LLM input, including any intermediate processing layers.\n46\t\n47\tOur key findings reveal a clean four-class taxonomy:\n48\t\\begin{enumerate}\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Immune}: Pure linear projection ($\\pi_0$) can tolerate 90\\% token removal with loss actually \\emph{improving} by 2.4\\%, because linearly projected tokens carry independent, uniformly distributed information (CV${}=0.038$).\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Threshold-graded}: Linear projection followed by deep post-processing (X-VLA, 12-layer BART encoder) is robust at $k \\geq 0.9$ (97.6\\% SR) but exhibits a cliff at $k=0.7$ (56.2\\% SR, $-43$pp). The BART encoder introduces partial token dependencies that create a pruning threshold.\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{Smooth graded}: Multi-layer MLP connectors (OpenVLA, 3-layer MLP with GELU) degrade monotonically ($k=0.5 \\to 37.4\\%$ SR, $-49\\%$ relative). Non-linear mixing creates partial dependencies, but tokens remain individually meaningful.\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Catastrophic}: Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA, $16\\times$ spatial folding) collapse at even 5\\% removal ($k=0.95 \\to 0\\%$ SR). Spatial folding creates irreversible cross-token entanglement.\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\tThe coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance scores serves as a zero-cost continuous predictor of pruning tolerance: lower CV indicates later onset of degradation. Critically, model size is \\emph{not} the explanatory variable---$\\pi_0$ (3.3B, linear) is immune while OpenVLA (7B, MLP) degrades significantly, and X-VLA (880M) shows intermediate behavior matching its intermediate CV.\n56\t\n57\tBased on this diagnostic analysis, we propose Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module ($\\sim$22M parameters) that learns to re-encode visual tokens into a compressed representation. On SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector, VTR recovers from 0\\% (hard pruning) to 60.1$\\pm$1.3\\% SR at 75\\% compression, with the M-curve confirmed flat across all compression ratios---demonstrating near-lossless compression for entangled tokens. We further show that VTR provides \\emph{universal} compression across connector types: on OpenVLA's MLP projector, VTC-only achieves 45\\% SR at 87.5\\% compression (M=32) and 52\\% SR at 75\\% compression (M=64), substantially outperforming hard pruning at equivalent compression rates.\n58\t\n59\t%% ============================================================\n60\t%% 2. BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK\n61\t%% ============================================================\n62\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n63\t\n64\t\\subsection{Vision-Language-Action Models}\n65\t\n66\tVLA models cast robotic manipulation as conditional text generation: given a language instruction and visual observation, an LLM backbone autoregressively generates discretized actions. The \\emph{vision connector} bridges the vision encoder (typically a frozen SigLIP or DINOv2) and the LLM by projecting image features into the LLM's token embedding space. This connector varies significantly across architectures:\n67\t\n68\t\\begin{itemize}\n69\t    \\item \\textbf{Pure linear projection} ($\\pi_0$): A single linear layer maps each vision patch to an LLM token. Tokens retain the spatial independence of the vision encoder's output.\n70\t    \\item \\textbf{Linear projection + deep post-processing} (X-VLA): Linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder that introduces cross-token mixing through self-attention.\n71\t    \\item \\textbf{Perceiver Resampler} (RoboFlamingo): Learnable cross-attention queries attend to vision features, producing a fixed set of aggregated tokens.\n72\t    \\item \\textbf{Multi-layer MLP} (OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT): A 3-layer MLP with GELU activations independently processes each token but introduces non-linear mixing within each token's representation.\n73\t    \\item \\textbf{Pixel-shuffle} (SmolVLA): $16\\times$ spatial folding rearranges 1024 patches into 64 tokens per camera by folding spatial dimensions into channels, creating extreme cross-token entanglement.\n74\t\\end{itemize}\n75\t\n76\t\\subsection{Token Reduction for Vision-Language Models}\n77\t\n78\tInference-time token reduction methods fall into two categories. \\emph{Token pruning} methods (FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O) remove tokens based on importance scores, typically derived from attention weights. \\emph{Token merging} methods (ToMe) combine similar tokens. Recent concurrent works---Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, and ``See What Matters''---all propose pruning specifically on MLP-projector VLAs. Our work differs by (1) explaining \\emph{why} pruning fails on pixel-shuffle architectures, (2) identifying \\emph{why} pruning succeeds on linear projections, and (3) proposing a diagnostic-guided re-encoding method motivated by cross-architecture analysis.\n79\t\n80\t%% ============================================================\n81\t%% 3. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n82\t%% ============================================================\n83\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Models}\n86\t\n87\tWe evaluate 6 VLA models spanning 4 connector families and sizes from 450M to 7B parameters (Table~\\ref{tab:models}).\n88\t\n89\t\\begin{table}[h]\n90\t\\centering\n91\t\\caption{VLA models evaluated, ordered by connector complexity.}\n92\t\\label{tab:models}\n93\t\\small\n94\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n95\t\\toprule\n96\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Connector} & \\textbf{Size} & \\textbf{Tokens} & \\textbf{Eval Mode} \\\\\n97\t\\midrule\n98\t$\\pi_0$ & Pure linear projection & 3.3B & 256 & Offline (loss) \\\\\n99\tRoboFlamingo & Perceiver Resampler & --- & --- & Closed-loop \\\\\n100\tX-VLA & Linear + BART 12L & 880M & 50 & Closed-loop \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BZJ5EkD2B6wW3wBsv37oJu","session_id":"b1a54116-c461-4e8a-8039-725147cd9263","uuid":"b26083c0-4b15-47fd-9345-bf06c3b73108","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:55:45.162Z"}
