{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PV6t3PHMrBdJoXswNLqQFg","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have read all the relevant materials thoroughly. Let me produce the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper presents a cross-architecture study of vision token pruning across five VLA models, discovering that the vision connector type determines pruning robustness along a spectrum from immune (linear projection) to catastrophic (pixel-shuffle). A CV-based diagnostic and a cross-attention re-encoding module (VTR) are proposed as a \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" framework. The paper is well-executed empirically but raises significant questions about the novelty and depth of its individual contributions, the completeness of the related work discussion, and whether the sum of parts reaches the CoRL acceptance bar.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The central finding is empirically clean and practically valuable.** The four-class taxonomy (immune / threshold-graded / smooth graded / catastrophic cliff) is supported by five models across three connector families, with the pi0-vs-OpenVLA controlled comparison (same 256 SigLIP tokens, different connector, 63pp gap) providing a convincing isolation of the causal factor. This is the paper's strongest novelty claim: no prior work has documented this architecture-dependent failure mode systematically.\n\n2. **The pixel-shuffle catastrophic failure is a genuinely important negative result.** The finding that removing just 5% of tokens on SmolVLA yields 0% SR, replicated across seven pruning configurations, two checkpoints, and three LIBERO suites, is robust and immediately actionable. Given the growing adoption of pixel-shuffle connectors in VLMs (InternVL, SmolVLA, TinyVLA), this has real deployment implications. The mechanistic explanation (spatial folding creates irreversible entanglement) is intuitive and correct.\n\n3. **Honest and thorough limitation disclosure.** The paper explicitly acknowledges pi0 is offline-only, reports the pruning method confound between models (random vs. L2 norm), notes VTR fails on OpenVLA (0% SR), and discusses the offline-to-closed-loop disconnect. This level of intellectual honesty is rare and appreciated.\n\n4. **Related work has improved substantially.** The bibliography now contains ~35 entries covering ViT pruning foundations (DynamicViT, ToMe, EViT), VLM token compression (FastV, LLaVA-PruMerge, TokenPacker, SparseVLM, SparseVILA), VLA-specific methods (VLA-Cache, VLA-IAP, EfficientVLA, VLA-Pruner), concurrent VLA compression works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters, Don't Run with Scissors), and vision connector foundations (Flamingo, BLIP-2, Perceiver, LLaVA, pixel-shuffle). This is a significant improvement over the 5-reference version described in the review prompt.\n\n5. **The confound exposures are useful contributions to the community.** Documenting the ~17pp gap between L2-norm and random pruning on OpenVLA, and the 28pp seed variance on X-VLA at k=0.7, provides important methodological caution for future VLA compression work.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) The pixel-shuffle failure is predictable from first principles, limiting novelty of the \"discovery.\"** Pixel-shuffle performs deterministic spatial folding: each output token is a channel-concatenation of a fixed 4x4 spatial block. By construction, these tokens carry non-redundant, spatially disjoint information. Removing any token eliminates an entire receptive field with no overlap or redundancy to compensate. This is an architectural invariant that can be deduced from reading the SmolVLA model card, without running any experiment. The paper's contribution is empirical confirmation of the predicted failure magnitude (exactly 0% SR even at 5% removal), which is useful but not a \"discovery\" in the intellectual sense. The paper would be strengthened by explicitly acknowledging the predictability and arguing that the contribution is the quantification and the demonstration that no pruning strategy (learned, random, geometric prior, merging) can overcome the structural constraint.\n\n2. **(Major) VTR novelty is minimal.** Cross-attention with learnable queries compressing a variable-length input to a fixed-length output is the exact architecture of Q-Former (BLIP-2, 2023), Perceiver Resampler (Flamingo, 2022), and numerous subsequent adaptations. The paper's VTR is a 2-layer, 12-head cross-attention module with M learnable queries -- this is architecturally identical to Q-Former. The claimed novelty is \"applying it to pixel-shuffle VLA token compression,\" but the more accurate framing is \"using the same learned compression that pixel-shuffle VLAs should have used from the start, as a post-hoc fix.\" The flat M-curve finding (M=16 approximately equals M=128) is interesting and suggests re-encoding rather than compression is the operative mechanism, but this is an observation about the pathology (pixel-shuffle tokens need reorganization, not reduction) rather than a novel method. VTR failing on OpenVLA (0% SR) further limits its generality.\n\n3. **(Major) The CV diagnostic has only 4 data points and no validation set.** The \"continuous spectrum\" claim (CV: 0.038 -> 0.151 -> 0.215 -> 0.314) is fitted on exactly 4 models and validated on zero held-out models. With 4 points, any monotone function would appear to \"predict\" the ordering. The paper calls this a \"zero-cost diagnostic,\" but a diagnostic tool requires validation on unseen architectures. The missing RoboFlamingo experiment (Perceiver Resampler) would have been a critical 5th point; its absence undermines the diagnostic claim. Is CV actually a useful predictor, or is it simply correlated with connector complexity in this particular 4-model sample? The paper should present this as a hypothesis, not a validated diagnostic.\n\n4. **(Minor) Pruning method confound undermines cross-model comparisons.** OpenVLA uses random removal (harder test), X-VLA uses L2-norm removal (easier test), and SmolVLA uses both. The paper acknowledges this and argues it \"strengthens\" findings because OpenVLA survives the harder test while X-VLA cliffs under the easier test. This is a reasonable argument, but it also means the quantitative comparisons (e.g., \"X-VLA k=0.7 = 57.2% vs OpenVLA k=0.7 = 49.0%\") are not apples-to-apples. The planned OpenVLA L2-norm experiments would partially address this, but they are listed as \"in progress\" and not in the paper.\n\n5. **(Minor) pi0 evaluation is offline only.** The paper's strongest architecture class (\"immune\") has zero closed-loop evidence. The X-VLA offline-to-closed-loop disconnect (offline loss 1.0014 at k=0.3, but SR drops to 23%) is documented in the same paper, directly undermining confidence in pi0's offline immunity claim. The paper acknowledges this in limitations, but \"immune\" is used throughout the main text without qualification until the footnotes. This is a labeling issue that could mislead readers.\n\n6. **(Minor) Single benchmark (LIBERO-Object).** The main results are all on one benchmark suite with 10 tasks. Cross-suite results (Spatial, Goal) are in the appendix for VTR only. No real-robot evaluation. For a CoRL venue paper making claims about \"deployment implications\" and \"design guidelines,\" the generalization evidence is thin.\n\n7. **(Minor) Contribution inflation.** The paper lists 4 contributions, but contributions 1 and 2 (taxonomy and CV diagnostic) are tightly coupled observations from the same analysis, and contribution 4 (\"offline metric disconnect\") is a well-known issue in robot learning (simulation-to-reality, offline-to-online gaps). The paper is fundamentally a 2-contribution work: (a) architecture-dependent pruning failure characterization, and (b) VTR as a partial fix for pixel-shuffle.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide a theoretical argument for why the CV threshold between \"immune\" and \"catastrophic\" would generalize beyond these 4 models? What CV value would you predict for InternVL's pixel-unshuffle connector (2x2 folding vs SmolVLA's 4x4)?\n\n2. The VTR M=128 passthrough gains +13pp over the SmolVLA baseline (44.8% -> 58.0%). This means VTR is not really a compression module but a feature reorganization module that happens to tolerate compression. Would a simpler approach -- inserting a 1x1 convolution or MLP before the LLM without any token reduction -- achieve similar gains?\n\n3. VTR yields 0% on OpenVLA even in passthrough mode (M=128). This is a catastrophic failure of VTR itself. Can you explain why adding a cross-attention layer destroys an already-functioning model? Does this suggest VTR requires careful integration (e.g., residual connections, gradual unfreezing) rather than being a generic \"plug-in\"?\n\n4. The pi0 \"immunity\" is based on offline loss only. Given X-VLA's dramatic offline-to-closed-loop gap, what is your confidence that pi0 would remain immune in closed-loop evaluation? Would you revise the \"immune\" label if closed-loop results showed degradation?\n\n5. How sensitive is the CV metric to the specific importance scoring method (L2 norm)? Have you computed CV using attention-based importance or gradient-based importance? If CV changes substantially with the scoring method, its value as a \"zero-cost diagnostic\" diminishes.\n\n6. You report 28pp seed variance for X-VLA at k=0.7. This is enormous. How do you reconcile reporting multi-seed means with 3 seeds when the variance is this large? Is 3 seeds sufficient to characterize the distribution?\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\nThe bibliography has improved significantly from the initial 5-reference version and now covers the major relevant areas. However, several important works and connections deserve discussion:\n\n- **H2O (Zhang et al., NeurIPS 2023)** and **StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., ICLR 2024)**: KV-cache eviction methods that relate to the token importance heterogeneity analysis. Both are in the literature map but not cited in the paper.\n- **Negative results literature**: Bouthillier et al. (ICML 2024, \"Embracing Negative Results\") would strengthen the framing as a cautionary tale. Present in the literature map but absent from the paper.\n- **DINOv2 (Oquab et al., TMLR 2024)**: Vision encoder used in OpenVLA; relevant to understanding why MLP-projected DINOv2+SigLIP tokens behave differently from linearly-projected SigLIP tokens. In the bib but not discussed in the connector analysis.\n- **Diffusion Policy (Chi et al., RSS 2023)**: Relevant context for flow matching action generation used by SmolVLA and pi0.\n- **The pixel-shuffle origin paper (Shi et al., CVPR 2016)** is cited but the connection to sub-pixel convolution in super-resolution and why the same operation creates problems in the VLA context could be developed more.\n- **TokenPacker (Li et al., 2024)**: Spatial-aware visual token compression that is somewhat related to VTR's approach. Cited in related work but the comparison could be deeper -- TokenPacker also uses cross-attention for spatial compression, so how does VTR differ?\n\n### Detailed Scores\n- Originality: 5/10\n- Quality: 7/10\n- Clarity: 8/10\n- Significance: 6/10\n- Relevance: 8/10\n- Limitations acknowledgment: 9/10\n\n### Overall Score: 5/10\n### Confidence: 4/5\n### Brief Justification\n\nThe paper's strongest asset is a thorough, honestly reported empirical study that documents an important architecture-dependent failure mode. However, the central \"discovery\" (pixel-shuffle fails under pruning) is largely predictable from first principles, VTR is architecturally identical to well-known cross-attention compression (Q-Former/Perceiver), the CV diagnostic has only 4 data points with no validation, and the primary evaluation model (pi0, the \"immune\" class) lacks closed-loop evidence. The paper reads as a competent empirical study that confirms intuitions rather than generating new ones. For CoRL's ~30% acceptance rate, the novelty bar is not met, though the work is clearly useful to the community. I would recommend a major revision addressing the closed-loop pi0 evaluation, a 5th+ connector type for CV validation, and a more honest framing of VTR as an application of known techniques rather than a novel method."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: abb8c7a4a054dc899 (use SendMessage with to: 'abb8c7a4a054dc899' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 87149\ntool_uses: 24\nduration_ms: 129021</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1a54116-c461-4e8a-8039-725147cd9263","uuid":"2c7e6f22-e807-4481-af75-4d07a6f306ca","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:57:17.796Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Produce a thorough, independent review.\n\n## Venue: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning)\n- Acceptance rate: ~30-35%\n- Review dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- Overall score scale (1-10): 10=seminal/top5%, 9=strong accept/top15%, 8=clear accept/top50%, 7=good accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below threshold, 4=not good enough, 3=clear reject, 2=strong reject, 1=trivial/wrong\n- Confidence scale (1-5): 5=absolutely certain, 4=confident, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to defend but not absolutely certain, 1=educated guess\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Token Pruning Catastrophically Fails on Pixel-Shuffle VLAs: Diagnosis and Recovery\"\n\n**Core claims**:\n1. Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA) catastrophically fail under ANY token pruning — 44.8% → 0% SR even removing just 5% of 128 tokens on LIBERO-Object\n2. Pure linear projection (π0) is immune — performance improves 2.4% even at 90% removal; CV=0.038\n3. CV-based zero-cost diagnostic predicts pruning tolerance from single forward pass (CV spectrum: 0.038 immune → 0.314 catastrophic)\n4. VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding, ~22M params, cross-attention) recovers pixel-shuffle VLAs from 0% → 60.1±1.3% at 75% compression; flat M-curve (M=16: 56.9% ≈ M=128: 58.0%)\n5. VTR fails on MLP-projector VLAs (OpenVLA): 0% SR across all M values — architecture-specific fix\n6. Pruning method confound: L2-norm vs random ~17pp systematic gap on OpenVLA\n7. Seed sensitivity: X-VLA k=0.7 shows 28pp variance across seeds (56.2% vs 28.0%)\n\n**4 listed contributions**:\n1. Catastrophic failure discovery (pixel-shuffle + pruning)\n2. Zero-cost CV diagnostic\n3. VTR recovery module\n4. Confound exposure (method + seed)\n\n**Models tested**: SmolVLA (450M, pixel-shuffle, 128 tokens), X-VLA (880M, linear+BART 12L, 50 tokens), π0 (3.3B, pure linear, 256 tokens), OpenVLA (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens), OpenVLA-OFT (7B, same MLP)\n\n**Benchmark**: LIBERO-Object only (10 tasks, 50 episodes/task, 500 total)\n\n**Related work covered in paper**:\n- Section 2.1: VLA models — cites OpenVLA [1], SmolVLA [2], π0 [3]\n- Section 2.2: Token pruning for VLMs/VLAs — cites attention-based [4], token reorganization [5], mentions 4 concurrent works: Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters\n- Only 5 references total in the bibliography\n\n**Key arguments about novelty**:\n- \"First systematic study of token pruning across VLA vision connector architectures\"\n- \"Four concurrent works all ignore pixel-shuffle VLAs\"\n- Claims the pixel-shuffle catastrophic failure finding \"alone has immediate practical implications\"\n- VTR is positioned as pixel-shuffle-specific recovery, not universal compression\n- The paper explicitly narrows scope to \"cautionary tale + diagnostic tool\"\n\n**VTR method**: Standard cross-attention with learnable queries (similar to Perceiver/Q-Former/Flamingo). The claimed novelty is applying it to pixel-shuffle VLA token compression and discovering that re-encoding (not compression) is the operative mechanism.\n\n**CV diagnostic**: Coefficient of variation of L2-norm token importance scores. Fitted on 4 models (π0=0.038, X-VLA=0.151, OpenVLA=0.215, SmolVLA=0.314). The paper claims this forms a \"continuous spectrum that predicts pruning tolerance.\"\n\n**Experiment context**: The paper was planned to include RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler) as a 5th connector type but this experiment was not completed. It's listed in Limitations as future work. This means the connector coverage is: linear, MLP, linear+deep-post-processing, pixel-shuffle — missing Perceiver-style.\n\n## Your Review Focus: Novelty and Related Work\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty of the central finding**: Is \"pixel-shuffle connectors fail under pruning\" surprising or predictable? Could this be deduced from first principles (spatial folding = information entanglement → pruning destroys)?\n2. **VTR novelty**: Cross-attention re-encoding is well-established (Perceiver, Q-Former, Flamingo). Is applying it to pixel-shuffle VLA token compression genuinely novel?\n3. **CV diagnostic novelty**: Is using CV of importance scores as a diagnostic novel or incremental? Only 4 data points — is this a \"diagnostic\" or just an observation?\n4. **Related work completeness**: Only 5 references! Is this sufficient? What important work is missing?\n   - Token merging (ToMe)\n   - FastV, LLaVA token compression\n   - Perceiver/Q-Former/Resampler literature\n   - Vision token compression in VLMs more broadly\n   - Other VLA efficiency works\n5. **Concurrent work engagement**: The paper claims 4 concurrent works miss pixel-shuffle. Is this positioning fair?\n6. **Contribution sizing**: Are 4 contributions justified, or is this primarily a single-finding paper (pixel-shuffle fails) with auxiliary observations?\n7. **Scope narrowing**: The paper explicitly frames itself as a \"cautionary tale\" with narrow but bulletproof claims. Is this scope sufficient for CoRL?\n8. **Theoretical depth**: Is the mechanistic explanation (spatial entanglement, per-token independence) sufficiently developed?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, specific and evidenced]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, specific and evidenced, with severity: major/minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be cited and discussed]\n\n### Detailed Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Limitations acknowledgment: X/10\n\n### Overall Score: X/10\n### Confidence: X/5\n### Brief Justification\n[2-3 sentences]\n```\n","agentId":"abb8c7a4a054dc899","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have read all the relevant materials thoroughly. Let me produce the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper presents a cross-architecture study of vision token pruning across five VLA models, discovering that the vision connector type determines pruning robustness along a spectrum from immune (linear projection) to catastrophic (pixel-shuffle). A CV-based diagnostic and a cross-attention re-encoding module (VTR) are proposed as a \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" framework. The paper is well-executed empirically but raises significant questions about the novelty and depth of its individual contributions, the completeness of the related work discussion, and whether the sum of parts reaches the CoRL acceptance bar.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The central finding is empirically clean and practically valuable.** The four-class taxonomy (immune / threshold-graded / smooth graded / catastrophic cliff) is supported by five models across three connector families, with the pi0-vs-OpenVLA controlled comparison (same 256 SigLIP tokens, different connector, 63pp gap) providing a convincing isolation of the causal factor. This is the paper's strongest novelty claim: no prior work has documented this architecture-dependent failure mode systematically.\n\n2. **The pixel-shuffle catastrophic failure is a genuinely important negative result.** The finding that removing just 5% of tokens on SmolVLA yields 0% SR, replicated across seven pruning configurations, two checkpoints, and three LIBERO suites, is robust and immediately actionable. Given the growing adoption of pixel-shuffle connectors in VLMs (InternVL, SmolVLA, TinyVLA), this has real deployment implications. The mechanistic explanation (spatial folding creates irreversible entanglement) is intuitive and correct.\n\n3. **Honest and thorough limitation disclosure.** The paper explicitly acknowledges pi0 is offline-only, reports the pruning method confound between models (random vs. L2 norm), notes VTR fails on OpenVLA (0% SR), and discusses the offline-to-closed-loop disconnect. This level of intellectual honesty is rare and appreciated.\n\n4. **Related work has improved substantially.** The bibliography now contains ~35 entries covering ViT pruning foundations (DynamicViT, ToMe, EViT), VLM token compression (FastV, LLaVA-PruMerge, TokenPacker, SparseVLM, SparseVILA), VLA-specific methods (VLA-Cache, VLA-IAP, EfficientVLA, VLA-Pruner), concurrent VLA compression works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, See What Matters, Don't Run with Scissors), and vision connector foundations (Flamingo, BLIP-2, Perceiver, LLaVA, pixel-shuffle). This is a significant improvement over the 5-reference version described in the review prompt.\n\n5. **The confound exposures are useful contributions to the community.** Documenting the ~17pp gap between L2-norm and random pruning on OpenVLA, and the 28pp seed variance on X-VLA at k=0.7, provides important methodological caution for future VLA compression work.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) The pixel-shuffle failure is predictable from first principles, limiting novelty of the \"discovery.\"** Pixel-shuffle performs deterministic spatial folding: each output token is a channel-concatenation of a fixed 4x4 spatial block. By construction, these tokens carry non-redundant, spatially disjoint information. Removing any token eliminates an entire receptive field with no overlap or redundancy to compensate. This is an architectural invariant that can be deduced from reading the SmolVLA model card, without running any experiment. The paper's contribution is empirical confirmation of the predicted failure magnitude (exactly 0% SR even at 5% removal), which is useful but not a \"discovery\" in the intellectual sense. The paper would be strengthened by explicitly acknowledging the predictability and arguing that the contribution is the quantification and the demonstration that no pruning strategy (learned, random, geometric prior, merging) can overcome the structural constraint.\n\n2. **(Major) VTR novelty is minimal.** Cross-attention with learnable queries compressing a variable-length input to a fixed-length output is the exact architecture of Q-Former (BLIP-2, 2023), Perceiver Resampler (Flamingo, 2022), and numerous subsequent adaptations. The paper's VTR is a 2-layer, 12-head cross-attention module with M learnable queries -- this is architecturally identical to Q-Former. The claimed novelty is \"applying it to pixel-shuffle VLA token compression,\" but the more accurate framing is \"using the same learned compression that pixel-shuffle VLAs should have used from the start, as a post-hoc fix.\" The flat M-curve finding (M=16 approximately equals M=128) is interesting and suggests re-encoding rather than compression is the operative mechanism, but this is an observation about the pathology (pixel-shuffle tokens need reorganization, not reduction) rather than a novel method. VTR failing on OpenVLA (0% SR) further limits its generality.\n\n3. **(Major) The CV diagnostic has only 4 data points and no validation set.** The \"continuous spectrum\" claim (CV: 0.038 -> 0.151 -> 0.215 -> 0.314) is fitted on exactly 4 models and validated on zero held-out models. With 4 points, any monotone function would appear to \"predict\" the ordering. The paper calls this a \"zero-cost diagnostic,\" but a diagnostic tool requires validation on unseen architectures. The missing RoboFlamingo experiment (Perceiver Resampler) would have been a critical 5th point; its absence undermines the diagnostic claim. Is CV actually a useful predictor, or is it simply correlated with connector complexity in this particular 4-model sample? The paper should present this as a hypothesis, not a validated diagnostic.\n\n4. **(Minor) Pruning method confound undermines cross-model comparisons.** OpenVLA uses random removal (harder test), X-VLA uses L2-norm removal (easier test), and SmolVLA uses both. The paper acknowledges this and argues it \"strengthens\" findings because OpenVLA survives the harder test while X-VLA cliffs under the easier test. This is a reasonable argument, but it also means the quantitative comparisons (e.g., \"X-VLA k=0.7 = 57.2% vs OpenVLA k=0.7 = 49.0%\") are not apples-to-apples. The planned OpenVLA L2-norm experiments would partially address this, but they are listed as \"in progress\" and not in the paper.\n\n5. **(Minor) pi0 evaluation is offline only.** The paper's strongest architecture class (\"immune\") has zero closed-loop evidence. The X-VLA offline-to-closed-loop disconnect (offline loss 1.0014 at k=0.3, but SR drops to 23%) is documented in the same paper, directly undermining confidence in pi0's offline immunity claim. The paper acknowledges this in limitations, but \"immune\" is used throughout the main text without qualification until the footnotes. This is a labeling issue that could mislead readers.\n\n6. **(Minor) Single benchmark (LIBERO-Object).** The main results are all on one benchmark suite with 10 tasks. Cross-suite results (Spatial, Goal) are in the appendix for VTR only. No real-robot evaluation. For a CoRL venue paper making claims about \"deployment implications\" and \"design guidelines,\" the generalization evidence is thin.\n\n7. **(Minor) Contribution inflation.** The paper lists 4 contributions, but contributions 1 and 2 (taxonomy and CV diagnostic) are tightly coupled observations from the same analysis, and contribution 4 (\"offline metric disconnect\") is a well-known issue in robot learning (simulation-to-reality, offline-to-online gaps). The paper is fundamentally a 2-contribution work: (a) architecture-dependent pruning failure characterization, and (b) VTR as a partial fix for pixel-shuffle.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide a theoretical argument for why the CV threshold between \"immune\" and \"catastrophic\" would generalize beyond these 4 models? What CV value would you predict for InternVL's pixel-unshuffle connector (2x2 folding vs SmolVLA's 4x4)?\n\n2. The VTR M=128 passthrough gains +13pp over the SmolVLA baseline (44.8% -> 58.0%). This means VTR is not really a compression module but a feature reorganization module that happens to tolerate compression. Would a simpler approach -- inserting a 1x1 convolution or MLP before the LLM without any token reduction -- achieve similar gains?\n\n3. VTR yields 0% on OpenVLA even in passthrough mode (M=128). This is a catastrophic failure of VTR itself. Can you explain why adding a cross-attention layer destroys an already-functioning model? Does this suggest VTR requires careful integration (e.g., residual connections, gradual unfreezing) rather than being a generic \"plug-in\"?\n\n4. The pi0 \"immunity\" is based on offline loss only. Given X-VLA's dramatic offline-to-closed-loop gap, what is your confidence that pi0 would remain immune in closed-loop evaluation? Would you revise the \"immune\" label if closed-loop results showed degradation?\n\n5. How sensitive is the CV metric to the specific importance scoring method (L2 norm)? Have you computed CV using attention-based importance or gradient-based importance? If CV changes substantially with the scoring method, its value as a \"zero-cost diagnostic\" diminishes.\n\n6. You report 28pp seed variance for X-VLA at k=0.7. This is enormous. How do you reconcile reporting multi-seed means with 3 seeds when the variance is this large? Is 3 seeds sufficient to characterize the distribution?\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\nThe bibliography has improved significantly from the initial 5-reference version and now covers the major relevant areas. However, several important works and connections deserve discussion:\n\n- **H2O (Zhang et al., NeurIPS 2023)** and **StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., ICLR 2024)**: KV-cache eviction methods that relate to the token importance heterogeneity analysis. Both are in the literature map but not cited in the paper.\n- **Negative results literature**: Bouthillier et al. (ICML 2024, \"Embracing Negative Results\") would strengthen the framing as a cautionary tale. Present in the literature map but absent from the paper.\n- **DINOv2 (Oquab et al., TMLR 2024)**: Vision encoder used in OpenVLA; relevant to understanding why MLP-projected DINOv2+SigLIP tokens behave differently from linearly-projected SigLIP tokens. In the bib but not discussed in the connector analysis.\n- **Diffusion Policy (Chi et al., RSS 2023)**: Relevant context for flow matching action generation used by SmolVLA and pi0.\n- **The pixel-shuffle origin paper (Shi et al., CVPR 2016)** is cited but the connection to sub-pixel convolution in super-resolution and why the same operation creates problems in the VLA context could be developed more.\n- **TokenPacker (Li et al., 2024)**: Spatial-aware visual token compression that is somewhat related to VTR's approach. Cited in related work but the comparison could be deeper -- TokenPacker also uses cross-attention for spatial compression, so how does VTR differ?\n\n### Detailed Scores\n- Originality: 5/10\n- Quality: 7/10\n- Clarity: 8/10\n- Significance: 6/10\n- Relevance: 8/10\n- Limitations acknowledgment: 9/10\n\n### Overall Score: 5/10\n### Confidence: 4/5\n### Brief Justification\n\nThe paper's strongest asset is a thorough, honestly reported empirical study that documents an important architecture-dependent failure mode. However, the central \"discovery\" (pixel-shuffle fails under pruning) is largely predictable from first principles, VTR is architecturally identical to well-known cross-attention compression (Q-Former/Perceiver), the CV diagnostic has only 4 data points with no validation, and the primary evaluation model (pi0, the \"immune\" class) lacks closed-loop evidence. The paper reads as a competent empirical study that confirms intuitions rather than generating new ones. For CoRL's ~30% acceptance rate, the novelty bar is not met, though the work is clearly useful to the community. I would recommend a major revision addressing the closed-loop pi0 evaluation, a 5th+ connector type for CV validation, and a more honest framing of VTR as an application of known techniques rather than a novel method."}],"totalDurationMs":129021,"totalTokens":87149,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":738,"cache_read_input_tokens":83355,"output_tokens":3053,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":738},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3053,"cache_read_input_tokens":83355,"cache_creation_input_tokens":738,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":738,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":12,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
