{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BZJ5EkD2B6wW3wBsv37oJu","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper presents the first cross-architecture study of vision token pruning across five VLA models (450M--7B) spanning three connector families, revealing a four-class taxonomy of pruning tolerance governed by effective vision-to-LLM pipeline complexity rather than model scale. The CV of token L2 norms is proposed as a zero-cost diagnostic, and Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) is introduced to recover performance on pixel-shuffle architectures where deletion catastrophically fails. The experimental work is substantial in breadth but has several methodological concerns around protocol heterogeneity, single-benchmark evaluation, and the strength of the CV diagnostic claim relative to the evidence provided.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Thorough SmolVLA ablation (Table 1/Appendix A).** Seven pruning configurations varying scoring (ACIS, random), retention ratio (k=0.50--0.95), and mechanism (zero-mask, true removal, ToMe merging) all produce 0% SR. Verification on the official checkpoint (59.2% baseline, same cliff) rules out undertrained-model artifacts. This is convincing evidence that the failure is architectural, not incidental.\n\n2. **Well-designed VTR controls.** The extended training control (45.0% SR, p>0.9 vs baseline) isolates the gain as architectural rather than from additional optimization. The parameter-matched MLP adapter (63.4% vs 60.1%, p=0.15) correctly identifies re-encoding as the operative mechanism rather than cross-attention specifically. The M=128 passthrough yielding +13pp over baseline is a clean demonstration that re-encoding adds value independent of compression.\n\n3. **3-seed evaluation for VTR claims (Table 4).** All five M values have 3-seed results, and the flat M-curve claim (M=16: 56.9+/-1.3 through M=128: 58.0+/-3.5) is well-supported with individual seed values reported. This is appropriate statistical practice for the paper's central method contribution.\n\n4. **Explicit acknowledgment of confounds.** The paper is transparent about the pruning method discrepancy (random for OpenVLA, L2-norm for X-VLA) and correctly argues that this asymmetry strengthens rather than weakens the architecture-dependent conclusion (OpenVLA survives the harder test; X-VLA cliffs under the easier test). The scale confound discussion (Section 6.1/Limitations) is honest and well-reasoned, citing the non-monotonic relationship between model size and pruning tolerance.\n\n5. **Pi0--OpenVLA controlled comparison.** Same SigLIP encoder lineage, same 256 token count, different connector. The 63pp gap at k=0.1 is one of the clearest pieces of evidence for the connector-as-causal-factor claim.\n\n6. **Per-task breakdowns (Appendix B).** These reveal important heterogeneity -- e.g., OpenVLA's butter and cream_cheese tasks collapse to 0% at k=0.3 while salad_dressing holds at 64%. This level of transparency strengthens reproducibility.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Heterogeneous pruning protocols undermine cross-model comparisons.** OpenVLA is evaluated under random token removal while X-VLA uses L2-norm-based removal. SmolVLA uses both but is 0% everywhere, so the comparison is moot for it. The paper argues the asymmetry strengthens the finding, which is partially correct, but it means the degradation curves in Figure 1a are not directly comparable -- we cannot overlay them on the same axes and draw conclusions about relative sensitivity. The planned L2-norm sweep on OpenVLA (mentioned in the project as D108) would have provided the needed apples-to-apples comparison. Without it, the quantitative differences between the \"smooth graded\" and \"threshold-graded\" classes are confounded with the pruning method. The authors should provide at least one overlapping condition (e.g., L2-norm pruning on OpenVLA at k=0.7 and k=0.5) to enable direct comparison.\n\n2. **(Major) Pi0 evaluation is offline only.** The paper's own Section 5.4 demonstrates that offline metrics dramatically underestimate closed-loop failure (X-VLA offline loss at k=0.3 is 1.0014, near-baseline, yet closed-loop SR is 23.0%, a -77pp drop). Given this explicit finding, claiming pi0 is \"immune\" based solely on offline L1 loss is internally contradictory. The pi0 \"improvement\" at k=0.1 could easily be an artifact of the same offline-closed-loop disconnect the paper documents. This is the paper's most prominent model (3.3B, linear projection, the \"ideal\" case), and its strongest claim rests on the weakest evidence. At minimum, the abstract and introduction should significantly downgrade the language from \"immune\" to something like \"offline-immune, pending closed-loop verification.\" Currently, the abstract states pi0 \"is immune\" without qualification.\n\n3. **(Major) Single benchmark (LIBERO-Object).** The primary results for all five models are on LIBERO-Object only (10 tasks, 500 episodes). Cross-suite results (Spatial, Goal) are reported only for SmolVLA VTR (Table 7). No other model is evaluated on Spatial or Goal under pruning. LIBERO-Object is a relatively simple tabletop manipulation benchmark. The taxonomy could be benchmark-specific: perhaps on harder tasks (LIBERO-Long, real-world), the \"smooth graded\" class becomes cliff-edge too, or the thresholds shift. The cross-suite VTR results actually hint at this -- Goal drops from 63.0% to 56.4% under VTR, suggesting task structure matters. The single-benchmark limitation is more severe for the CV diagnostic claim: we have no evidence that the same CV thresholds predict behavior on other benchmarks.\n\n4. **(Major) CV diagnostic rests on N=4 data points.** The CV spectrum (0.038, 0.151, 0.215, 0.314) maps to four pruning behaviors -- but this is a monotonic relationship with exactly four observations. Any monotonic function through four points will appear to \"predict\" the ordering. The paper cannot distinguish between \"CV is a principled continuous predictor\" and \"we happened to observe four models whose CV ordering matches their pruning ordering.\" This is acknowledged nowhere. To validate CV as a diagnostic, the authors would need either (a) more models with distinct CV values that fall on the predicted curve, or (b) a synthetic experiment showing CV predicts pruning tolerance when other factors are controlled. The RoboFlamingo Perceiver Resampler experiment (listed in Limitations as future work) would have added a fifth, architecturally distinct data point. Without it, the diagnostic claim is descriptive rather than predictive.\n\n5. **(Minor) SmolVLA baseline underperformance.** The fine-tuned SmolVLA baseline is 44.8% vs the official 87.3% (or 59.2% on the authors' reproduction of the official checkpoint). The 2x gap is attributed to batch size (4 vs 512). This is a large gap that raises questions about whether the fine-tuned model is a representative instantiation of the architecture. While the official checkpoint shows the same cliff-edge (confirming the qualitative finding), all VTR experiments use the 44.8% baseline. The VTR gain (44.8% -> 60.1%) could partly reflect the re-encoding module compensating for undertrained features, as the authors themselves note. On the official checkpoint, VTR actually decreases performance (59.2% -> 53.4%), which is a concerning result that should be discussed more prominently. It suggests VTR may not generalize to well-trained models.\n\n6. **(Minor) X-VLA has only 50 tokens; extreme compression is unusual.** X-VLA starts with only 50 vision tokens, so k=0.7 means retaining 35 tokens and k=0.3 means retaining 15. The \"cliff at k=0.7\" removes only 15 tokens. The practical relevance of pruning a 50-token model is limited compared to pruning 256-token models (OpenVLA, pi0). The X-VLA result is theoretically interesting but may not generalize to the regime where token pruning is practically useful.\n\n7. **(Minor) 100K training steps for VTR -- convergence not demonstrated.** The paper reports 100K steps with batch size 4 on a single 4090. No learning curves or convergence analysis are shown. Given the M=32 train loss (0.11) vs M=64 train loss (0.045), the training dynamics differ substantially across configurations. The M=96 dip (54.5% vs 62.0% for M=64, p=0.034) could be a convergence issue rather than an intrinsic bottleneck. The paper's explanation (\"intermediate bottleneck that introduces representational redundancy\") is speculative. Extended training for M=96 would resolve this.\n\n8. **(Minor) Wilson CIs vs t-test inconsistency.** Single-seed results use Wilson 95% CIs (appropriate for proportions), but multi-seed results use mean +/- std (n=3). With n=3, the std-based intervals are not confidence intervals in the frequentist sense and substantially understate uncertainty. Proper 95% CIs with n=3 would be approximately +/-4.3 * std (t-distribution with 2 df), which would be much wider than the reported +/- std values.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you run L2-norm pruning on OpenVLA at any retention ratio? Even a single data point (e.g., k=0.7) would allow direct comparison with X-VLA under the same protocol.\n\n2. The VTR on the official SmolVLA checkpoint (59.2% -> 53.4%) represents a 5.8pp *decrease*. Does this mean VTR is only beneficial for undertrained models? How should practitioners interpret this for deployment?\n\n3. For the CV diagnostic: if a new VLA model has CV=0.18 (between X-VLA's 0.151 and OpenVLA's 0.215), what specific prediction does the framework make about its pruning curve? Is the relationship expected to be linear in CV?\n\n4. X-VLA's per-task variance at k=0.7 is extreme (cream_cheese: 0%, bbq_sauce: 2% vs. alphabet_soup: 94%, butter: 100%). Does this pattern hold across seeds? If some tasks reliably collapse while others reliably survive, the \"threshold-graded\" label may obscure task-specific cliff-edges.\n\n5. Why was the VTR experiment on OpenVLA run for only 20K steps when SmolVLA VTR used 100K? Is it possible that 100K steps would yield non-zero SR?\n\n6. The M=128 passthrough (+13pp over baseline) is surprising -- it means the cross-attention module improves SmolVLA even without compression. Is this gain from the additional 22.2M parameters, the cross-attention architecture, or the additional 100K training steps? The extended training control (45.0%) addresses the third possibility, but was this control trained with the same number of trainable parameters?\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n1. **Pi0 closed-loop evaluation on LIBERO.** This is the single most impactful missing experiment. The paper's own findings (Section 5.4) invalidate offline-only evaluation. Pi0 has LIBERO evaluation code available. Even a single k value (e.g., k=0.5) in closed-loop would dramatically strengthen or appropriately bound the \"immune\" claim.\n\n2. **L2-norm pruning on OpenVLA.** At least k=0.7 and k=0.5 under L2-norm selection, to enable apples-to-apples comparison with X-VLA.\n\n3. **A fifth model for CV diagnostic validation.** RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler) was planned but not completed. Any additional VLA model with a different CV value would strengthen the diagnostic claim beyond N=4 curve fitting.\n\n4. **VTR on a second architecture.** VTR on OpenVLA failed (0% SR at 20K steps, M=128). The VTC-only M=32/M=64 experiments are listed as \"in progress\" in the project but do not appear in the paper. Including these would clarify whether VTR failure on MLP projectors is fundamental or a training issue.\n\n5. **Cross-benchmark evaluation.** Even one additional benchmark (e.g., LIBERO-Long, or a different manipulation suite) for the pruning taxonomy would address the single-benchmark concern.\n\n6. **Ablation on pixel-shuffle fold factor.** The paper attributes the cliff-edge to 4x4 spatial folding. Testing 2x2 folding (if architecturally feasible) would provide direct evidence for this mechanistic claim.\n\n### Detailed Scores\n- Originality: 7/10\n- Quality: 5/10\n- Clarity: 7/10\n- Significance: 6/10\n- Relevance: 8/10\n- Limitations acknowledgment: 8/10\n\n### Overall Score: 5/10\n### Confidence: 4/5\n### Brief Justification\nThe paper identifies a genuinely important phenomenon (architecture-dependent pruning sensitivity in VLAs) and the SmolVLA cliff-edge finding is robust and well-documented. However, the experimental methodology has significant gaps that undermine the paper's broader claims. The heterogeneous pruning protocols make cross-model comparisons imprecise; the pi0 \"immune\" claim contradicts the paper's own finding that offline metrics are unreliable; the CV diagnostic is fit to N=4 points with no validation; and all results are on a single, relatively simple benchmark. The VTR method is well-controlled for SmolVLA but the 0% on OpenVLA and the performance decrease on the official SmolVLA checkpoint limit its practical scope. The paper is currently a solid empirical observation (pixel-shuffle breaks under pruning; MLP projectors don't) wrapped in claims that outrun the evidence (universal taxonomy, continuous diagnostic, \"immune\" class). Tightening the claims to match the evidence, adding pi0 closed-loop evaluation, and providing one unified pruning protocol comparison would likely push this to a 6-7."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aad06c96b8b878d75 (use SendMessage with to: 'aad06c96b8b878d75' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 85214\ntool_uses: 26\nduration_ms: 163701</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1a54116-c461-4e8a-8039-725147cd9263","uuid":"14625de4-a475-45de-a5ca-db7aee3855c9","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:57:24.861Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce a thorough, independent review.\n\n## Venue: CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning)\n- Acceptance rate: ~30-35%\n- Review dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- Overall score scale (1-10): 10=seminal/top5%, 9=strong accept/top15%, 8=clear accept/top50%, 7=good accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below threshold, 4=not good enough, 3=clear reject, 2=strong reject, 1=trivial/wrong\n- Confidence scale (1-5): 5=absolutely certain, 4=confident, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to defend but not absolutely certain, 1=educated guess\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Token Pruning Catastrophically Fails on Pixel-Shuffle VLAs: Diagnosis and Recovery\"\n\n**Core claims**:\n1. Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA) catastrophically fail under ANY token pruning — 44.8% → 0% SR even removing just 5% of 128 tokens on LIBERO-Object\n2. Pure linear projection (π0) is immune — performance improves 2.4% even at 90% removal; CV=0.038\n3. CV-based zero-cost diagnostic predicts pruning tolerance from single forward pass (CV spectrum: 0.038 immune → 0.314 catastrophic)\n4. VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding, ~22M params, cross-attention) recovers pixel-shuffle VLAs from 0% → 60.1±1.3% at 75% compression; flat M-curve (M=16: 56.9% ≈ M=128: 58.0%)\n5. VTR fails on MLP-projector VLAs (OpenVLA): 0% SR across all M values — architecture-specific fix\n6. Pruning method confound: L2-norm vs random ~17pp systematic gap on OpenVLA\n7. Seed sensitivity: X-VLA k=0.7 shows 28pp variance across seeds (56.2% vs 28.0%)\n\n**Models tested**: SmolVLA (450M, pixel-shuffle, 128 tokens), X-VLA (880M, linear+BART 12L, 50 tokens), π0 (3.3B, pure linear, 256 tokens), OpenVLA (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens), OpenVLA-OFT (7B, same MLP)\n\n**Benchmark**: LIBERO-Object only (10 tasks, 50 episodes/task, 500 total). Wilson 95% CIs.\n\n**Key tables**:\n- Table 1: SmolVLA all pruning methods → 0% across all k values\n- Table 2: OpenVLA random vs L2-norm: systematic ~17pp gap at every k\n- Table 3: X-VLA cross-seed: k=0.7 seed42=56.2% vs seed44=28.0% (Δ=−28.2pp)\n- Table 4: SmolVLA VTR M-curve, 3-seed: all M values 54.5-62.0%, flat\n- Table 5: OpenVLA VTR all M values → 0% (action collapse)\n- Table 6: CV diagnostic summary across 5 models\n\n**VTR details**: Cross-attention with M learnable queries, VTC-only training (frozen LLM), 100K steps. SmolVLA 3-seed eval. Also tested MLP adapter (~22.14M params) achieving 63.4±2.6% at M=32 (p=0.15 vs cross-attention 60.1%), confirming re-encoding is the mechanism not specific architecture. Cross-suite generalization: Spatial 53.2%, Goal 56.4%.\n\n**π0 evaluation**: Offline only (normalized action-prediction loss), not closed-loop.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned but may not all appear in paper)\nKey experiments that were planned:\n- OpenVLA L2-norm pruning full sweep (k=0.95/0.9/0.7/0.5/0.3) — ALL appear in paper Table 2\n- X-VLA cross-seed at k=0.7 (seed44) and k=0.9 (seed44) — appear in Table 3\n- OpenVLA cross-seed random pruning (seed43) at k=0.7 and k=0.5 — appear as text in Section 5.3\n- OpenVLA VTR (VTC-only) M=32/64/96/128 — appear in Table 5\n- SmolVLA VTR full M-sweep 3-seed — appear in Table 4\n- RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler connector) — NOT completed, listed in Limitations as future work\n- SmolVLA VTR cross-suite generalization — mentioned in text\n\n## Your Review Focus: Methods and Experiments\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Experimental methodology soundness**: Is the pruning protocol fair? Are baselines adequate? Is 500 episodes sufficient?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are CIs appropriate? Is 3-seed evaluation sufficient for VTR claims? Is single-seed okay for some results?\n3. **VTR method design**: Is the cross-attention re-encoding well-motivated? Is VTC-only training justified? Is 100K steps enough?\n4. **CV diagnostic validity**: Is CV of L2-norm a principled diagnostic? Only 4 data points on the CV spectrum — is this sufficient?\n5. **Confound analysis**: Is the L2-norm vs random comparison fair and well-executed?\n6. **Missing experiments**: What critical experiments are absent?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Is enough detail provided?\n8. **π0 offline-only evaluation**: How much does this weaken the claims?\n9. **Single benchmark (LIBERO-Object only)**: How limiting is this?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, specific and evidenced]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, specific and evidenced, with severity: major/minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Detailed Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Limitations acknowledgment: X/10\n\n### Overall Score: X/10\n### Confidence: X/5\n### Brief Justification\n[2-3 sentences]\n```\n","agentId":"aad06c96b8b878d75","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper presents the first cross-architecture study of vision token pruning across five VLA models (450M--7B) spanning three connector families, revealing a four-class taxonomy of pruning tolerance governed by effective vision-to-LLM pipeline complexity rather than model scale. The CV of token L2 norms is proposed as a zero-cost diagnostic, and Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) is introduced to recover performance on pixel-shuffle architectures where deletion catastrophically fails. The experimental work is substantial in breadth but has several methodological concerns around protocol heterogeneity, single-benchmark evaluation, and the strength of the CV diagnostic claim relative to the evidence provided.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Thorough SmolVLA ablation (Table 1/Appendix A).** Seven pruning configurations varying scoring (ACIS, random), retention ratio (k=0.50--0.95), and mechanism (zero-mask, true removal, ToMe merging) all produce 0% SR. Verification on the official checkpoint (59.2% baseline, same cliff) rules out undertrained-model artifacts. This is convincing evidence that the failure is architectural, not incidental.\n\n2. **Well-designed VTR controls.** The extended training control (45.0% SR, p>0.9 vs baseline) isolates the gain as architectural rather than from additional optimization. The parameter-matched MLP adapter (63.4% vs 60.1%, p=0.15) correctly identifies re-encoding as the operative mechanism rather than cross-attention specifically. The M=128 passthrough yielding +13pp over baseline is a clean demonstration that re-encoding adds value independent of compression.\n\n3. **3-seed evaluation for VTR claims (Table 4).** All five M values have 3-seed results, and the flat M-curve claim (M=16: 56.9+/-1.3 through M=128: 58.0+/-3.5) is well-supported with individual seed values reported. This is appropriate statistical practice for the paper's central method contribution.\n\n4. **Explicit acknowledgment of confounds.** The paper is transparent about the pruning method discrepancy (random for OpenVLA, L2-norm for X-VLA) and correctly argues that this asymmetry strengthens rather than weakens the architecture-dependent conclusion (OpenVLA survives the harder test; X-VLA cliffs under the easier test). The scale confound discussion (Section 6.1/Limitations) is honest and well-reasoned, citing the non-monotonic relationship between model size and pruning tolerance.\n\n5. **Pi0--OpenVLA controlled comparison.** Same SigLIP encoder lineage, same 256 token count, different connector. The 63pp gap at k=0.1 is one of the clearest pieces of evidence for the connector-as-causal-factor claim.\n\n6. **Per-task breakdowns (Appendix B).** These reveal important heterogeneity -- e.g., OpenVLA's butter and cream_cheese tasks collapse to 0% at k=0.3 while salad_dressing holds at 64%. This level of transparency strengthens reproducibility.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Heterogeneous pruning protocols undermine cross-model comparisons.** OpenVLA is evaluated under random token removal while X-VLA uses L2-norm-based removal. SmolVLA uses both but is 0% everywhere, so the comparison is moot for it. The paper argues the asymmetry strengthens the finding, which is partially correct, but it means the degradation curves in Figure 1a are not directly comparable -- we cannot overlay them on the same axes and draw conclusions about relative sensitivity. The planned L2-norm sweep on OpenVLA (mentioned in the project as D108) would have provided the needed apples-to-apples comparison. Without it, the quantitative differences between the \"smooth graded\" and \"threshold-graded\" classes are confounded with the pruning method. The authors should provide at least one overlapping condition (e.g., L2-norm pruning on OpenVLA at k=0.7 and k=0.5) to enable direct comparison.\n\n2. **(Major) Pi0 evaluation is offline only.** The paper's own Section 5.4 demonstrates that offline metrics dramatically underestimate closed-loop failure (X-VLA offline loss at k=0.3 is 1.0014, near-baseline, yet closed-loop SR is 23.0%, a -77pp drop). Given this explicit finding, claiming pi0 is \"immune\" based solely on offline L1 loss is internally contradictory. The pi0 \"improvement\" at k=0.1 could easily be an artifact of the same offline-closed-loop disconnect the paper documents. This is the paper's most prominent model (3.3B, linear projection, the \"ideal\" case), and its strongest claim rests on the weakest evidence. At minimum, the abstract and introduction should significantly downgrade the language from \"immune\" to something like \"offline-immune, pending closed-loop verification.\" Currently, the abstract states pi0 \"is immune\" without qualification.\n\n3. **(Major) Single benchmark (LIBERO-Object).** The primary results for all five models are on LIBERO-Object only (10 tasks, 500 episodes). Cross-suite results (Spatial, Goal) are reported only for SmolVLA VTR (Table 7). No other model is evaluated on Spatial or Goal under pruning. LIBERO-Object is a relatively simple tabletop manipulation benchmark. The taxonomy could be benchmark-specific: perhaps on harder tasks (LIBERO-Long, real-world), the \"smooth graded\" class becomes cliff-edge too, or the thresholds shift. The cross-suite VTR results actually hint at this -- Goal drops from 63.0% to 56.4% under VTR, suggesting task structure matters. The single-benchmark limitation is more severe for the CV diagnostic claim: we have no evidence that the same CV thresholds predict behavior on other benchmarks.\n\n4. **(Major) CV diagnostic rests on N=4 data points.** The CV spectrum (0.038, 0.151, 0.215, 0.314) maps to four pruning behaviors -- but this is a monotonic relationship with exactly four observations. Any monotonic function through four points will appear to \"predict\" the ordering. The paper cannot distinguish between \"CV is a principled continuous predictor\" and \"we happened to observe four models whose CV ordering matches their pruning ordering.\" This is acknowledged nowhere. To validate CV as a diagnostic, the authors would need either (a) more models with distinct CV values that fall on the predicted curve, or (b) a synthetic experiment showing CV predicts pruning tolerance when other factors are controlled. The RoboFlamingo Perceiver Resampler experiment (listed in Limitations as future work) would have added a fifth, architecturally distinct data point. Without it, the diagnostic claim is descriptive rather than predictive.\n\n5. **(Minor) SmolVLA baseline underperformance.** The fine-tuned SmolVLA baseline is 44.8% vs the official 87.3% (or 59.2% on the authors' reproduction of the official checkpoint). The 2x gap is attributed to batch size (4 vs 512). This is a large gap that raises questions about whether the fine-tuned model is a representative instantiation of the architecture. While the official checkpoint shows the same cliff-edge (confirming the qualitative finding), all VTR experiments use the 44.8% baseline. The VTR gain (44.8% -> 60.1%) could partly reflect the re-encoding module compensating for undertrained features, as the authors themselves note. On the official checkpoint, VTR actually decreases performance (59.2% -> 53.4%), which is a concerning result that should be discussed more prominently. It suggests VTR may not generalize to well-trained models.\n\n6. **(Minor) X-VLA has only 50 tokens; extreme compression is unusual.** X-VLA starts with only 50 vision tokens, so k=0.7 means retaining 35 tokens and k=0.3 means retaining 15. The \"cliff at k=0.7\" removes only 15 tokens. The practical relevance of pruning a 50-token model is limited compared to pruning 256-token models (OpenVLA, pi0). The X-VLA result is theoretically interesting but may not generalize to the regime where token pruning is practically useful.\n\n7. **(Minor) 100K training steps for VTR -- convergence not demonstrated.** The paper reports 100K steps with batch size 4 on a single 4090. No learning curves or convergence analysis are shown. Given the M=32 train loss (0.11) vs M=64 train loss (0.045), the training dynamics differ substantially across configurations. The M=96 dip (54.5% vs 62.0% for M=64, p=0.034) could be a convergence issue rather than an intrinsic bottleneck. The paper's explanation (\"intermediate bottleneck that introduces representational redundancy\") is speculative. Extended training for M=96 would resolve this.\n\n8. **(Minor) Wilson CIs vs t-test inconsistency.** Single-seed results use Wilson 95% CIs (appropriate for proportions), but multi-seed results use mean +/- std (n=3). With n=3, the std-based intervals are not confidence intervals in the frequentist sense and substantially understate uncertainty. Proper 95% CIs with n=3 would be approximately +/-4.3 * std (t-distribution with 2 df), which would be much wider than the reported +/- std values.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you run L2-norm pruning on OpenVLA at any retention ratio? Even a single data point (e.g., k=0.7) would allow direct comparison with X-VLA under the same protocol.\n\n2. The VTR on the official SmolVLA checkpoint (59.2% -> 53.4%) represents a 5.8pp *decrease*. Does this mean VTR is only beneficial for undertrained models? How should practitioners interpret this for deployment?\n\n3. For the CV diagnostic: if a new VLA model has CV=0.18 (between X-VLA's 0.151 and OpenVLA's 0.215), what specific prediction does the framework make about its pruning curve? Is the relationship expected to be linear in CV?\n\n4. X-VLA's per-task variance at k=0.7 is extreme (cream_cheese: 0%, bbq_sauce: 2% vs. alphabet_soup: 94%, butter: 100%). Does this pattern hold across seeds? If some tasks reliably collapse while others reliably survive, the \"threshold-graded\" label may obscure task-specific cliff-edges.\n\n5. Why was the VTR experiment on OpenVLA run for only 20K steps when SmolVLA VTR used 100K? Is it possible that 100K steps would yield non-zero SR?\n\n6. The M=128 passthrough (+13pp over baseline) is surprising -- it means the cross-attention module improves SmolVLA even without compression. Is this gain from the additional 22.2M parameters, the cross-attention architecture, or the additional 100K training steps? The extended training control (45.0%) addresses the third possibility, but was this control trained with the same number of trainable parameters?\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n1. **Pi0 closed-loop evaluation on LIBERO.** This is the single most impactful missing experiment. The paper's own findings (Section 5.4) invalidate offline-only evaluation. Pi0 has LIBERO evaluation code available. Even a single k value (e.g., k=0.5) in closed-loop would dramatically strengthen or appropriately bound the \"immune\" claim.\n\n2. **L2-norm pruning on OpenVLA.** At least k=0.7 and k=0.5 under L2-norm selection, to enable apples-to-apples comparison with X-VLA.\n\n3. **A fifth model for CV diagnostic validation.** RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler) was planned but not completed. Any additional VLA model with a different CV value would strengthen the diagnostic claim beyond N=4 curve fitting.\n\n4. **VTR on a second architecture.** VTR on OpenVLA failed (0% SR at 20K steps, M=128). The VTC-only M=32/M=64 experiments are listed as \"in progress\" in the project but do not appear in the paper. Including these would clarify whether VTR failure on MLP projectors is fundamental or a training issue.\n\n5. **Cross-benchmark evaluation.** Even one additional benchmark (e.g., LIBERO-Long, or a different manipulation suite) for the pruning taxonomy would address the single-benchmark concern.\n\n6. **Ablation on pixel-shuffle fold factor.** The paper attributes the cliff-edge to 4x4 spatial folding. Testing 2x2 folding (if architecturally feasible) would provide direct evidence for this mechanistic claim.\n\n### Detailed Scores\n- Originality: 7/10\n- Quality: 5/10\n- Clarity: 7/10\n- Significance: 6/10\n- Relevance: 8/10\n- Limitations acknowledgment: 8/10\n\n### Overall Score: 5/10\n### Confidence: 4/5\n### Brief Justification\nThe paper identifies a genuinely important phenomenon (architecture-dependent pruning sensitivity in VLAs) and the SmolVLA cliff-edge finding is robust and well-documented. However, the experimental methodology has significant gaps that undermine the paper's broader claims. The heterogeneous pruning protocols make cross-model comparisons imprecise; the pi0 \"immune\" claim contradicts the paper's own finding that offline metrics are unreliable; the CV diagnostic is fit to N=4 points with no validation; and all results are on a single, relatively simple benchmark. The VTR method is well-controlled for SmolVLA but the 0% on OpenVLA and the performance decrease on the official SmolVLA checkpoint limit its practical scope. The paper is currently a solid empirical observation (pixel-shuffle breaks under pruning; MLP projectors don't) wrapped in claims that outrun the evidence (universal taxonomy, continuous diagnostic, \"immune\" class). Tightening the claims to match the evidence, adding pi0 closed-loop evaluation, and providing one unified pruning protocol comparison would likely push this to a 6-7."}],"totalDurationMs":163701,"totalTokens":85214,"totalToolUseCount":26,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":853,"cache_read_input_tokens":80959,"output_tokens":3399,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":853},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3399,"cache_read_input_tokens":80959,"cache_creation_input_tokens":853,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":853,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014qqFoqkeMgbnQT4pAJRZgN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the full roundtable:\n\n**Step 1: Independent reviews** — Done (R1: 5/10, R2: 5/10)\n\n**Step 2: Cross-share** — R1 and R2 share their reviews and identify where they agree/disagree\n\n**Step 3: Converge** — Discussion and convergence on key points\n\n**Step 4: Synthesize** — Final consolidated review report\n\nLet me now proceed with Steps 2-4 and then submit the review. I'll do this by synthesizing myself based on the two reviews.\n\n\nLooking at where the reviewers align and diverge, they both converge on the core issues: the offline-only evaluation, the weak CV diagnostic with minimal data points, and the single benchmark limitation. They also both recognize the pixel-shuffle finding as solid and flag the pruning method heterogeneity as a confound. Where they split is in emphasis—R1 pushes harder on methodological rigor gaps like missing L2-norm analysis and training convergence verification, while R2 questions whether the pixel-shuffle result is truly novel given what first principles would predict.\n\nThe bigger tension is on novelty and quality: R1 sees more originality (7/10) but lower execution quality (5/10), whereas R2 flips this—lower novelty (5/10) because VT is essentially Q-Former, but higher quality (7/10) in what's actually there. R1 also surfaces the VTR checkpoint performance drop that R2 doesn't mention, and R2 flags the contribution inflation problem more explicitly. Now I need to synthesize these into a coherent roundtable discussion and structure the final report with the consensus scores.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3156,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7821,"cache_read_input_tokens":55434,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_